\title{Learning across diseases and outcomes for haematological risk prediction}
\author{\placeholder{Authors and affiliations}}
\date{Working manuscript --- \today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Disease-specific clinical prediction fragments a large patient population into
many disease--outcome problems with uneven label support. We ask three
questions: whether supervised learning across diseases and outcomes improves
prediction, whether those models outperform strong task-specific tabular
models, and whether externally pretrained representations add value beyond
local pretraining, before and after local domain adaptation. The study uses a
linked Danish haematology resource with 24 fine disease cohorts, 69 approved
outcomes and six prediction horizons. A matched supervision factorial compares
one disease--outcome specialist, one model per outcome across diseases, one
model per disease across outcomes, and a fully joint model. Patient-dependent
source routing is tested separately against the fully joint shared model.
External-direct and external-plus-domain-adapted encoders are compared under
fixed shared and selected-router readouts. Model selection uses 2022 tuning
data; patients indexed from 2023 onward form the held-out evaluation.
\placeholder{Insert locked effects for pooling, XGBoost comparison, external
pretraining and domain adaptation, including uncertainty and negative transfer.}
The design distinguishes the availability of shared supervision from its use
by a particular architecture and reports where transfer succeeds or fails.
\end{abstract}

\section*{Introduction}
Prediction informs supportive care, surveillance and treatment planning in
haematology. Even a large linked resource can contain few relevant events when
patients are divided by disease, target outcome and prediction horizon.
Disease-specific scores compress a limited diagnostic snapshot into risk
strata \citep{zhou2014nccnipi,cllipi2016}. Task-specific machine learning can
use more clinical history, but typically fits a separate predictor from an
aggregated feature table \citep{agius2020clltim,werling2026dlbcl}. Neither
approach automatically supplies supervision to a low-support target.

Two different resources may help. First, labelled patients in other diseases
and other outcomes may inform the target through shared supervised learning
\citep{harutyunyan2019multitask,zhang2019metapred}. These two axes need not
have the same value: observing the same outcome in another disease differs
from observing another outcome in the same disease. Related clinical labels
also do not guarantee positive transfer. Pooling can improve some prediction
problems while worsening others.

Second, self-supervised pretraining can learn representations from patient
histories before target labels are supplied
\citep{li2020behrt,rasmy2021med,odgaard2024core,steinberg2023motor}.
Population-scale pretraining offers broader experience than a local resource,
but the benefit must be measured against what can be learned locally and
against strong tabular comparators \citep{wornow2023ehrshot,wornow2023shaky}.
Local domain-adaptive pretraining is an additional intervention: it may change
the value of the external representation and its interaction with downstream
sharing. External initialization and local adaptation must therefore be
compared separately.

We organize supervised learning as a disease--outcome matrix
(\cref{fig:supervision-grid}). Diseases form rows and outcomes form columns.
A cell specialist receives one cell's labels; a cross-disease model receives
one column; a cross-outcome model receives one row; and the fully joint model
receives the whole available panel. Holding pretraining and the shared
architecture fixed, this factorial measures the contribution of each pooling
axis and their interaction. A separate comparison asks whether conditional
routing over disease and outcome adapters improves on ordinary joint learning.
Both shared BCE and the router receive the same fully populated training
panel; switching an adapter bank off does not remove its supervision.

DALY-CARE links Danish national registers, hospital records, laboratory data
and disease-specific quality registries \citep{brieghel2025dalycare}. Its
variation in disease frequency and endpoint support provides a natural setting
for examining the three questions. Our earlier aggressive-lymphoma study
motivated cross-disease learning for treatment failure
\citep{werling2026dlbcl}; the present study uses a different, broader first-line
population and evaluates both pooling axes across the approved panel. We
report matched effects, negative transfer and unsupported cells rather than
requiring the most complex model to win.

