\subsection*{Disease and outcome pooling contribute distinct sources of supervision}
Using the same local encoder and shared prediction architecture, the
supervision factorial estimates disease pooling without other outcomes
($D-S$), outcome pooling within a disease ($O-S$), disease pooling conditional
on outcome pooling ($J-O$), and outcome pooling conditional on disease pooling
($J-D$). The combined effect is $J-S$; the interaction is $J-D-O+S$ on the
chosen metric scale. Each comparison uses the same target patients, labels,
eligibility and horizon. These contrasts measure training procedures that
change available supervision, not pure biological relationships at equal
compute.
\placeholder{Insert paired held-out pooling effects and uncertainty, the
relative contributions of the two axes, their interaction and coverage.}

We examine whether these effects vary with target-cell training events,
patient support, disease, endpoint group and horizon. Raw paired estimates
remain visible beside hierarchical summaries. Cells without an estimable
specialist contribute a separate coverage result and do not receive invented
specialist contrasts. Natural rarity describes where gains occur; it does not
establish the effect of experimentally reducing labels.

