\subsection*{Conditional routing is evaluated above ordinary joint learning}
The selected router is compared with J under the same supervision and
training protocol. A matched source-bank factorial compares neither bank,
disease sources, outcome sources and both banks. Both-minus-disease estimates
the outcome bank's incremental value; outcome-only resembling neither does
not rule out that conditional effect. The source-only arms retain the disease
and target query context and the entire supervised panel.
\placeholder{Insert selected source bank, query mode, rank, paired router--J
and source-bank effects, uncertainty and unsuccessful extensions.}

Outcome routing is retained only if its incremental value is supported on
tuning; disease-only routing or the shared model remains a valid final choice.
Rank, query structure and query width are distinct choices assessed in a
bounded tuning screen. Gate mass describes the allocation of corrections, not
predictive contribution. Source removal redistributes the remaining gate
weights, so its effect is a post-hoc sensitivity analysis. A claim that learned
cross-source allocation causes the gain requires a separately trained
fixed/home-only-routing comparator.

\subsection*{Neural prediction is compared with strong task-specific tabular models}
The selected local predictor, shared J and independent S are compared with
train-fitted, tuning-selected EHR XGBoost on the same target populations.
J versus XGBoost measures what is available without routing; S versus XGBoost
assesses the sequence/pretraining pipeline without pooled supervision. These
are practical pipeline comparisons, not isolated architecture effects with
identical inputs. Logistic regression provides a tabular reference;
registry-enriched predictors and clinical scores are reported on separately
matched complete-case populations.
\placeholder{Insert locked neural--XGBoost effects, absolute performance,
calibration, heterogeneity and cells favoring the tabular comparator.}

