\subsection*{External pretraining and local domain adaptation are separate interventions}
Three encoder origins are compared under two fixed readouts: local256 (L),
external-direct (E), and the same external initialization after local DAPT
(EA), each with shared J and the locally selected router R. E versus L
estimates the external-initialization package effect; EA versus E estimates
the added local adaptation stage; EA versus L compares the full adapted
external pipeline with local pretraining. Direct E still receives supervised
training: only local self-supervised adaptation is absent.
\placeholder{Insert verified external source provenance, direct export,
DAPT completion and paired E--L, EA--E and EA--L effects under both readouts.}

The difference in router-minus-J effects across origins measures
pretraining-by-routing interaction; comparing that difference between EA and
E measures DAPT-by-routing interaction. Downstream rank/query/source choices
remain fixed, avoiding a new architecture search for each encoder. The
reduced design does not establish whether external pretraining changes the
value of supervised pooling itself. That stronger question requires the
S/D/O/J factorial under the external origin, on a prespecified subset or the
full panel depending on the intended claim.

