\subsection*{Required secondary analyses locate benefits and failures}
Tuning-defined strata identify cells with negative J--S effects, then
held-out router--J and router--S effects assess whether routing mitigates that
harm. Patient-level paired probability-loss contributions are summarized by
pre-index history density, age, sex and baseline-defined atypicality. DAPT
contrasts are examined against train-defined domain mismatch and alongside
calibration. Calendar/horizon analyses, specialist coverage and a
\emph{tuning-selected} S/D/O bank versus one pooled model assess robustness
and operational value. Computational cost and directional source-removal
sensitivity accompany performance where those exports exist.
\placeholder{Insert prespecified secondary findings, missing inputs,
uncertainty and sensitivity to excluding rare later-coded diagnosis cases.}
These analyses reuse locked predictions; they do not select subgroups,
strategies or checkpoints using held-out outcomes.

\section*{Discussion}
This study separates three questions often combined in a foundation-model
comparison: whether labelled tasks should be learned together, whether that
learning improves on strong tabular prediction, and whether broader
self-supervised experience improves what can be learned locally. The
supervision matrix makes disease and outcome pooling explicit, while the
readout and encoder comparisons change different parts of the pipeline.
\placeholder{Insert the three principal findings and the most informative
negative result after the held-out analysis is locked.}

A benefit from multi-outcome training does not require outcome-source adapters
to improve performance: the shared encoder already receives gradients from
other outcomes. Conversely, a router improvement does not by itself identify
biological task relationships, because explicit disease conditioning and added
capacity are part of the architecture. The study therefore permits a simpler
selected model and distinguishes predictive, mechanistic and operational claims.

External pretraining is evaluated as a transferable initialization rather than
assumed superior because it is larger. Local adaptation may address vocabulary
or care-system differences, supply extra self-supervised optimization, or
change the usefulness of downstream sharing. Its contribution is measured
against the same direct external initialization, not inferred from EA--L alone.
Where architecture, source population, objective or token budget differ,
external-versus-local results concern the pretraining package rather than scale
in isolation.

Partial pooling helps summarize noisy effects across a large task panel, but
clinical hierarchy and smoothness are assumptions, not evidence of relatedness.
The raw paired effects and patient-bootstrap uncertainty remain the empirical
foundation. Hierarchical summaries are interpreted alongside simpler models,
prior/grouping sensitivities and predictive checks. Natural rarity cannot
replace a controlled label-scarcity experiment, and posterior convergence does
not certify the sampling-error dependence model.

Limitations include the single healthcare context, changes in coding and
measurement, endpoint-specific ascertainment, correlated outcomes and shared
evaluation patients across seeds. Some endpoints are CTCAE-inspired numerical
thresholds or administrative proxies rather than fully observed clinical
grades. Rare later-coded diagnoses plausibly reflect clinical coding delay;
the exact subtype's availability at treatment requires provenance and clinical
review. The population and index reconstruction differ from earlier lymphoma
studies, so cross-paper performance changes are not direct replications.

\section*{Methods}

\subsection*{Population, endpoints and prediction origin}
DALY-CARE links national registers and hospital/quality-registry data
\citep{brieghel2025dalycare}. The index is reconstructed first-line treatment.
\placeholder{Insert approved extract dates, governance, cohort flow and
current Table 1.} Each patient has one index across configured outcomes.
Clinical inputs are truncated strictly before the index timestamp. Disease
identity is separately audited as a prediction-time input: the cohort builder
allows diagnosis-coding grace periods of 14 days and forward rescue through
90 days. Disease-specific treatment before formal registration supports a
coding-delay interpretation, but does not automatically establish every fine
subtype. Date-lag counts and the same-fit sensitivity excluding later-coded
cases are reported without silently redefining the cohort.

The 69-endpoint release retains documented eligibility, baseline adequacy,
source coverage, competing-event and follow-up definitions. Unascertainable
outcomes are missing supervision, not negatives. Primary events after observed
follow-up are not treated as observed. Nonfatal endpoints retain competing
death; endpoints already containing death are modelled without a second
competing cause. Numerical CTCAE-inspired endpoints and antimicrobial-use
proxies are named with their definition caveats. Full code lists and risk-set
counts are supplementary.

\subsection*{Temporal split and local encoder}
Supervised training uses index dates through 2021, tuning uses 2022, and
held-out evaluation uses index dates from 2023. Patient-level membership is
shared across endpoints. The physical random 90/10 subject stores govern
self-supervised fitting/validation; they are pooled before applying the
prospective supervised split. Pretraining uses events strictly before
1 January 2022. Vocabulary and numeric metadata are fitted on the permitted
SSL-training view and frozen. Pre-cutoff histories of subsequently treated
patients can participate, so the claim concerns future treatment episodes,
not necessarily entirely unseen individuals.

The running local candidate uses a 256-wide native BONSAI encoder, four layers
and four heads, scaled Time2Vec and context length 8,192. The harmonized local
release uses categorical joined-bin numeric tokens. Autoregressive pretraining
uses chronological event-complete windows and retains a validation-selected
checkpoint after the recorded training budget. \placeholder{Insert actual
saved architecture, vocabulary hash, epochs/token budget and compute audit.}
The earlier 64-dimensional experiments are supplementary matched work; the
primary comparisons require the new 256-dimensional initialization.

\subsection*{Supervision factorial and direct prediction}
S/D/O/J use the same selected local encoder, trainable backbone and shared
linear interval-hazard heads. Each target produces primary and competing
hazards whose cumulative incidence is evaluated at 30, 60, 90, 180, 365 and
730 days. Direct multi-horizon BCE uses known labels at each horizon: primary
events are positive from event time onward, competing death is a known
negative, and administrative censoring before a horizon leaves status unknown.
Clinical grade variants retain the approved endpoint-group loss weighting.
Structural cohort/endpoint exclusions remain in force.

The planned budget is 30 complete epochs with natural sampling and matched
optimizer/batch settings across scopes. Support curriculum is off in this
factorial. S starts from the pretrained encoder, without pooled supervised
weights. D fits one outcome across all applicable fine diseases; O fits all
applicable outcomes within one fine disease; J fits the whole panel. Horizons
do not multiply the training count. Equal epochs do not equate optimizer steps,
label exposure or compute, which are recorded separately.

\subsection*{Conditional source routing}
Let $h_i$ be the pre-index patient representation and $k$ the target outcome.
The readout forms
\begin{equation}
 h_{ik}=h_i+\lambda\sum_s\pi_{iks}c_s A_s(h_i),
 \qquad c_s=\frac{e_s}{e_s+\tau},
 \label{eq:router}
\end{equation}
where $A_s$ is a low-rank nonlinear residual adapter, $e_s$ is its training
support proxy, and $\pi$ mixes available sources with a shared-route logit.
The patient remains an additive baseline regardless of shared gate mass.
Support counts are training-only; disease counts pool events across endpoints
and are not directly equivalent to outcome-bank counts. Shrinkage is a support
heuristic, not an estimated posterior credibility weight.

The query uses patient, disease and target identity. The additive query sums
projected vectors; the interacting query adds an MLP of their concatenation.
Target identity selects the prediction task, not its observed value. Both
contexts remain in active source-only arms. Home floors retain the available
home source; unknown fine identity uses the shared path. Anchored adapters
receive parameter updates only from home supervision, whereas the borrowed
variant permits cross-target updates. These are different supervised ownership
rules, not different access to held-out outcomes. The construction relates to
gated experts and parameter-efficient adapters
\citep{jacobs1991adaptive,ma2018mmoe,houlsby2019adapters,pfeiffer2021adapterfusion}.

The reference settings are rank8, residual scale0.7, home floor0.05, event
tau10 and shared-logit initialization0. Source choice, rank8 versus16,
additive versus interacting query, and query128 versus512 are assessed in a
bounded tuning sequence. \placeholder{Insert final policy, source bank,
rank/query capacity, adapter epsilon and initialization hash.} Shared/router
comparisons match curriculum and the common probability-loss checkpoint rule.
Earlier scale/architecture screens are reported separately. Learned-borrowing
claims require trained fixed/home-only controls; removal inference alone is
not that counterfactual.

\subsection*{External initialization and local domain adaptation}
The external checkpoint and original vocabulary undergo architecture,
code-system, value/time and temporal-provenance audits. Encoder tensors are
strictly loaded, common vocabulary rows are copied by token string, and
local-only rows receive a recorded seeded initialization. Source prediction
heads and optimizer state are excluded. The direct E export performs no local
SSL fitting. EA begins from that same remapped export and continues SSL on the
permitted local training records, with SSL validation selection and no
supervised held-out labels. Thus remapping initialization is held fixed in
EA--E. \placeholder{Insert source population/date coverage, unmatched-token
frequencies and DAPT objective/budget/provenance.}

J and the locally locked R are fitted for L/E/EA with matched downstream
protocols. External architectures are not retuned through the full local
router search. Pretraining-by-pooling is an optional prespecified subset
extension, not implied by this six-fit downstream matrix.

\subsection*{Tabular comparators}
EHR feature matrices use the same patient-specific prediction origin.
Preprocessing and imputation fit training data only; XGBoost and logistic
hyperparameters minimize tuning log loss within each target cell and horizon.
The matched binary comparisons use the same known-status CIF labels and
ascertainment eligibility as neural horizon BCE, including competing deaths
as known negatives and excluding early-censored unknown status. Outcome,
horizon and prospective split definitions match neural evaluation. Cell-specific models know the
disease through model choice; pooled tabular comparators receive the same
legitimate baseline disease information. Registry-enriched predictors and
clinical scores use separately matched complete-case populations. IPI-style
rank scores are not treated as calibrated probabilities.

\subsection*{Selection, estimands and paired uncertainty}
All new neural comparison checkpoints minimize complete-tuning horizon BCE,
averaging patients within known horizon status, then horizons, grades and
endpoint groups. Single-class tuning cells can select on probability loss;
zero known tuning evidence cannot. Each pooled fit supplies one selected
checkpoint to all evaluation cells. Architectural choices, support criteria,
aggregation, calibration rules and seed roster are fixed before held-out
outcomes are inspected. \placeholder{Insert final practical tolerance,
formal comparison family and support thresholds.}

Primary population-risk reporting uses the matching CIF/IPCW AUC and Brier
estimands. Required secondary binary analyses use the explicitly identified
known-status population and report its exclusions; unweighted BCE/log loss
is not called censoring-adjusted population risk. Patients are resampled
jointly across methods, endpoints and horizons, and the same evaluation-patient
draw is reused across training seeds. Seed-averaged intervals condition on the
fitted seed roster; seed variation and pretraining-seed uncertainty are
reported separately. Unsupported or failed specialists remain in coverage
summaries. Task-balanced and population-weighted summaries are distinct.

\subsection*{Hierarchy for effect heterogeneity}
Paired contrasts are the foundation; hierarchical analysis summarizes where
effects vary with target-cell training support. A crossed disease/outcome/cell
model with a linear log-support trend is the reference. A smooth spline and
clinical parent-group nesting are assessed through bounded sensitivity models,
metric-scaled priors and prior/posterior predictive checks. Raw effects remain
visible. Within each question family, independent paired effects are fitted
jointly and conditional/combined contrasts are deterministic posterior sums.
For example, disease and outcome pooling and their interaction determine both
paths from a specialist to the fully joint model. Horizons and metrics are
analysed separately. Training-seed effects are averaged within shared patient
bootstrap draws before fitting. A multivariate Normal likelihood uses their
estimated covariance across contrasts and endpoints within each fine disease,
with prespecified diagonal shrinkage and a numerical variance floor. The
finite-panel posterior average uses declared disease/endpoint-group weights;
it is distinct from the mean rarity curve. Posterior probabilities of meaningful
benefit, harm and practical equivalence use a metric-specific tolerance.
Clinical nesting and spline flexibility are sensitivity assumptions, and
convergence does not establish predictive adequacy. The old independent-error
rarity spline is retained only for historical reproduction.
\placeholder{Insert locked priors/tolerances, covariance sensitivity,
validated predictive checks and real-data sampling diagnostics.}

\subsection*{Secondary analyses and reproducibility}
Negative-transfer strata and the best S/D/O strategy are defined on tuning,
then evaluated on held-out predictions. Patient subgroups use pre-index or
train-only definitions. No held-out recalibration or subgroup selection is
performed. All fits retain resolved configurations, source checkpoints,
release/vocabulary hashes, seeds and completion provenance. Prediction and
bootstrap artifacts remain on protected storage; only reviewed aggregates
enter figures. Software/run readiness and analysis definitions are documented
separately from empirical findings.

